  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 3,002 feet, <strong>Bradley Peak</strong> is a summit in Alaska. The Bradley Peak Trail covers 26.1 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 411 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. From the trailhead at about 52 feet, the route tops out near 394 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=60.9856,-149.50179\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">60.9856, -149.50179</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Adelie Point, Penguin Peak and Chinstrap Peak \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
